anyon like this. ","caption":"Easy Canyon","poi_name":null,"subject":"Chris Ennis","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-06-22T17:28:55.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-30T17:28:55.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/EpLxiCTSqxGVZRpGevAKm","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/EpLxiCTSqxGVZRpGevAKm"}},{"id":"ZBH3HB5unG861dab8ywqI","ext":"jpg","post_id":"w39lqePe2TNeDCgXF7egD","description":"Just below the Logging Creek Bridge this view offers a glimpse of the impressive limestone scenery downstream. ","caption":"Logging Creek","poi_name":null,"subject":"Chris Ennis","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":"dY2QgB1ZmhHXzPJQZqYDB","photo_date":"2023-06-22T17:28:55.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-30T17:28:55.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/ZBH3HB5unG861dab8ywqI","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/ZBH3HB5unG861dab8ywqI"}},{"id":"DwbMwIyqnUzX5he2EObAJ","ext":"jpg","post_id":"w39lqePe2TNeDCgXF7egD","description":"Cobble rapids abound above Logging Creek. ","caption":"Upper Rapids","poi_name":null,"subject":"Chris Ennis","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-06-22T17:28:55.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-30T17:28:55.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/DwbMwIyqnUzX5he2EObAJ","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/DwbMwIyqnUzX5he2EObAJ"}},{"id":"41199","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"10019","description":"","caption":"Sluice Box Canyon","poi_name":"","subject":"Belt Creek","author":"Ryan  Forde","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/41199","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/41199"}},{"id":"885697","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"888482","description":"","caption":"Belt Creek","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/885697","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/885697"}},{"id":"0aeDKuWdyFe7gcMy8iae4","ext":"jpg","post_id":"lYbH9PYHfXdoEamYfhiEw","description":"Fast flow, quite manageable on packrafts","caption":"730 CFS with packrafts in May 2025","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Lukas Eddy","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/0aeDKuWdyFe7gcMy8iae4","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/0aeDKuWdyFe7gcMy8iae4"}},{"id":"GtNJ2FFPxublTxIVGXDXx","ext":"jpg","post_id":"lYbH9PYHfXdoEamYfhiEw","description":"At 730cfs, there are only maybe two rapids Class III- that need scouting, and are quite doable by packraft","caption":"Scouting the gorge before committing","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Lukas Eddy","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/GtNJ2FFPxublTxIVGXDXx","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/GtNJ2FFPxublTxIVGXDXx"}},{"id":"XPQcPWuEYlmUMi8nPzJjc","ext":"jpg","post_id":"lYbH9PYHfXdoEamYfhiEw","description":"","caption":"","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Lukas Eddy","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-05-26T18:52:16.000Z","updated_at":"2025-05-26T18:52:17.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/XPQcPWuEYlmUMi8nPzJjc","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/XPQcPWuEYlmUMi8nPzJjc"}},{"id":"pzdrYsv2nl6YrlZI18isO","ext":"jpg","post_id":"lYbH9PYHfXdoEamYfhiEw","description":"Plenty of fun in packrafts and drysuits at 730cfs in May 2025","caption":"Flat but very swift water at 730cfs","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Lukas Eddy","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","updated_at":"2025-05-26T18:54:36.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/pzdrYsv2nl6YrlZI18isO","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/pzdrYsv2nl6YrlZI18isO"}},{"id":"42432","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"9132","description":"into the heart of the sluice boxes","caption":"Into the Sluiceboxes","poi_name":"","subject":"D-Man","author":"Dave Amman","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/42432","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/42432"}},{"id":"CwtR8Od0Lm7Xv09gnCCT8","ext":"jpg","post_id":"w39lqePe2TNeDCgXF7egD","description":"There was one nice surf wave midway down the run. ","caption":"Surfs Up!","poi_name":null,"subject":"Chris Ennis","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-06-22T17:28:55.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-30T17:28:55.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/CwtR8Od0Lm7Xv09gnCCT8","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/CwtR8Od0Lm7Xv09gnCCT8"}},{"id":"XGyiCKclNPaMthAFiGCah","ext":"jpg","post_id":"w39lqePe2TNeDCgXF7egD","description":"Belt Creek does not require a hike-in but is still a great run for packrafts and duckies. The tight cobble bar rapids at the top are particularly well suited to being able to bounce over stuff and hop out as needed. ","caption":"Packrafting Gold","poi_name":null,"subject":"Willow Colburn","author":"Kevin Colburn","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2023-06-22T17:28:55.000Z","updated_at":"2023-06-30T17:28:55.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/XGyiCKclNPaMthAFiGCah","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/XGyiCKclNPaMthAFiGCah"}}],"detail":{"id":"981","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"Belt Creek is absolutely beautiful, a mini- [Smith River](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1021_) with beautiful limestone walls that tower over the run, and eventually lead to a couple-mile long vertical walled box canyon a couple miles above the takeout. The run most often starts in Monarch, where the river is small and flows over braided cobble bars that can hold log jams and brush. Be careful of wood in this section. The scenery gradually ramps up as thw limestone walls grow in stature and the river gains more organized Class II and -III rapids. You should watch out for a few class 3's above the canyon (which starts about the last 3rd of the float), especially at the entrance but pretty much stay center. The canyon is awesome, swift pushy water could lead to a bunch of wall collisions, but not a serious danger at lower water when the walls feel a bit undercut but are relatively easy to avoid. Paddlers caution about higher flows because of the funky hydraulics and chances of a log jam, though experienced paddlers run it high. Belt Creek takes around 4-8 hrs, depending on flow and stops.\n\nThe [Neihart section](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/982_) is just upstream and has been run at high flows in IK's when 'it's definitely a ride.'\n\nBelt Creek flows through [Sluice Boxes State Park](http://stateparks.mt.gov/sluice-boxes/) as well as a mix of private property. Please be respectful of private property, and follow State Park rules. Belt Creek has little to no paddling infrastructure (bathrooms, formal launches, etc), so please make every effort to leave no trace in this special river canyon.\n\n(Based on an initial Dustin Prinzing report from 2008-05-27)","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"16","correlations":[{"reachID":"981","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":"150 is barely navigable in kayaks","lowRunnableRangeComment":"350 is a nice low level","mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":"Very pushy over 1000","lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"